● Phases A through E of the multi-tenant isolation plan are fully implemented,
  tested, and passing across the whole repo. Summary of what landed:

  Phase A — model/tenant.go (new Tenant model + CRUD), TenantId columns on
  User/Channel/Token/Log/Ability, registered in both AutoMigrate lists, UserBase
  cache carries TenantId.

  Phase B — session/token auth threads tenant_id into request context;
  middleware.EffectiveTenantId/AssertSameTenant helpers added next to
  AdminAuth/RootAuth.

  Phase C — channel/ability selection (DB path and memory-cache path),
  Distribute()'s explicit-channel-id and affinity paths, and the model-catalog
  listing functions all enforce tenant_id IN (0, caller).

  Phase D — GetAllUsers/SearchUsers, GetAllChannels/SearchChannels/SearchTags,
  GetAllLogs all gained tenant filters (Admin forced to own tenant, Root
  optional/unfiltered); CreateUser/AddChannel/CopyChannel force-assign tenant
  for non-root callers; UpdateUser/UpdateChannel tenant reassignment is
  root-only; single-channel ops
  (GetChannel/UpdateChannel/DeleteChannel/TestChannel/CopyChannel) enforce
  AssertSameTenant (tenant-admins can also touch shared channels, per your
  earlier answer).

  Phase E — controller/tenant.go (root-only CRUD) + /api/tenant router group
  gated by RootAuth().

  One pre-existing safety net caught a real gap along the way:
  controller/channel_authz_test.go's field-classification guard failed until I
  classified the new tenant_id field as sensitive in channel_authz.go —
  otherwise a ChannelWrite-only admin could have silently reassigned a channel's
  tenant.

  Two things intentionally left out of this pass (not in the approved plan's
  explicit scope, flagging for visibility): DeleteDisabledChannel (bulk
  endpoint) and GetLogsStat (usage aggregate) still operate cross-tenant. Worth
  a follow-up if full isolation needs to extend there.

