  Verification: Multi-tenant isolation (backend Phases A–E + frontend Phase F)

  Verdict: PASS (after fixing one real bug found during verification)

  Claim: Tenants can be created/managed by Root; users and channels can be
  assigned to a tenant; a tenant-scoped Admin sees and can act on only their own
  tenant's private resources plus shared ones; Root retains full cross-tenant
  visibility; UI elements (Tenant Management page, tenant pickers) are gated
  correctly by role.

  Method: Cold start (no project run/verify skill existed yet). Built the Go
  backend, seeded a fresh SQLite DB directly via a throwaway script (bypassing
  the multi-step setup wizard) with a Root user and an Admin user, launched the
  backend on :13001 and the rsbuild frontend dev server on :3000 (proxying /api
  to the backend), then drove it with Playwright (Chromium, freshly installed)
  through the real authenticated browser session — clicking through drawers, and
  for a few specific authorization claims, calling the real API via fetch()
  inside that same authenticated page context.

  Steps

  1. ✅ Logged in as Root (verifyroot), navigated to /tenants → page renders
  with sidebar entry, empty state "No tenants yet", Create Tenant button.
  2. ✅ Created two tenants ("Acme Corp", "Globex Inc") through the actual
  Create-Tenant drawer → both appear in the table with success toasts, IDs,
  status badges.
  3. ❌→✅ Assigned the seeded Admin user to "Acme Corp" via the Update-User
  drawer's Tenant picker (Root-only, correctly rendered) → UI showed "User
  updated successfully," but the database still showed tenant_id=0. Found a real
  bug: UpdateUser's EditWithTx call does a trailing tx.First(user, user.Id)
  refetch that overwrites the in-memory updatedUser struct — including TenantId
  — with the stale pre-update DB row, before the tenant-reassignment code read
  it. The reassignment silently became a no-op that still reported success.
  Fixed by capturing the client-requested tenant_id into a local variable before
  EditWithTx runs (controller/user.go). Added a regression test
  (TestUpdateUser_RootReassignsTenant_ActuallyPersists) that fails against the
  old code and passes now. Re-ran the exact same UI flow against the rebuilt
  binary → DB now correctly shows tenant_id=1.
  4. ✅ Logged in as the now tenant-scoped Admin (verifyadmin) → sidebar has no
  "Tenant Management" entry; direct navigation to /tenants redirects to /403;
  Users list shows only themself (Root, tenant 0, correctly excluded);
  Create-User drawer has no Tenant field.
  5. ✅ Seeded one shared channel (tenant_id=0) and one private channel per
  tenant. As Acme's admin, the Channels page shows exactly "Shared-OpenAI" +
  "Acme-Private" (2 total) — "Globex-Private" never appears.
  6. ✅ As Root, the Channels page shows all 3 channels.
  7. ✅ As Acme's admin, GET + PUT on the shared channel (id=1) both succeed
  (200) — confirms the confirmed policy that tenant-admins may edit shared
  channels, not just their own.
  8. 🔍 As Acme's admin, direct fetch() GET on Globex's private channel (id=3,
  bypassing the UI entirely) → 403 {"message":"无权访问该渠道"}.
  9. 🔍 As Acme's admin, direct fetch() PUT on Globex's private channel → same
  403, confirming the check applies to writes too, not just reads.
  10. 🔍 As Acme's admin, direct fetch() DELETE on Globex's private channel →
  403, but with a different message ("Unauthorized, insufficient privileges") —
  traced this to a pre-existing, unrelated ChannelSensitiveWrite Casbin
  permission gate at the router level that this seeded admin doesn't have,
  blocking the request before my tenant check ever runs. Still correctly
  blocked, but noted so I don't over-claim which code path was exercised.

